<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hidalgo (state)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hidalgo is a state of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a>. It contains the archaeological site of Tula, capital of the Toltecs in the Early Postclassic period, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of Pachuca and Real del Monte, worked since the colonial period. The state was created in 1869 and is named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a>, leader of the independence rising of 1810.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hidalgo-state.jpg" alt="Hidalgo (state)" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thelmadatter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CanyonSouthPe%C3%B1aHuasca.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hidalgo is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> on the north, Puebla on the east, Tlaxcala and the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on the south and Querétaro on the west. It covers roughly 20,813 square kilometres and is divided into 84 municipalities. Its terrain is very rugged, running from a few metres above sea level to more than 3,300 metres. It has three geographical regions: the Atlantic Coastal Plain, the Sierra Madre Oriental and the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt, which holds the highest peaks. The wet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a> area lies near the Gulf Coast, and the very northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> lies within the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Prehistoric artifacts have been found at Tepeapulco, Tulancingo, Actopan and Huichapan, dating from about 14,000 to 2,500 BCE, and the oldest evidence of occupation is at Huapalcalco in Tulancingo. Obsidian was worked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-de-las-navajas">Sierra de las Navajas</a> during the Preclassic period, and the first evidence of social stratification in the region comes from La Loma. The area lay within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, and many peoples passed through it from the north, often settling in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>. The Toltecs first settled at Xochicoatlán, now Molango, at the beginning of the 7th century, and later had their capital Tollan, today Tula.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> created the State of Hidalgo in 1869 and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a> its capital. The suffix de Soto was added later to honour Manuel Fernando Soto, regarded as the chief driving force behind the state's creation. Its name honours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla</a>, who began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a>. Mining has shaped its population: when mining activity fell in the mid-20th century the population fell as well, and recovery began in the 1980s. Cornish miners arrived in the 19th century in the Pachuca and Real del Monte areas, and their descendants keep the Protestant faith in many cases and the Cornish pasty, called pastes, as a signature dish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The state's population is 2.858 million. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a> and Tepehua each still speak their own language; the Otomi are the largest group, with over 250,000 speakers in the state. The Mezquital Valley preserves much of the Otomi culinary heritage, including edible insects such as escamoles, and the state dish is barbacoa. Its attractions include the ruins of Tula, thermal springs, old mining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> and mountain ranges. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a> is nicknamed the Beautiful Windy City because of its strong winds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hidalgo (state)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
